F1 formal's clause: "not exists q in I+ with γ ⊆ J^-(q)" = γ is not contained in any single J^-(q).

These are different: SET allows γ covered by the union but not by a single J^-(q). Canonical tail: some tail in a single J^-(q).

So F1's quantifiers.formal encodes the negation of the *canonical negation* under a "single q whole curve" reading = "no single q contains the whole curve". In terms of hiddenness, that is weaker than canonical (canonical says no single q contains a tail; a whole-curve non-containment allows a tail contained). It's also different from SET (SET's union). And per F0 line 189-193, using the set-based wording/reading is explicitly warned against ("the two readings must never be interchanged"), and using the whole-curve single-q form is a third reading not registered anywhere. This is a strong class-binding defect.

